<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>multi-module</artifactId>
    <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <!--
    Aggregator for the payment bounded context. Like inventory it has no HTTP surface: it
    reacts to ordering's PaymentRequested integration event and answers with its own. Its
    authorization decision is a pure rule, but it is not stateless: it keeps an at-most-once
    operation-dedupe log behind the PaymentOperations port. That log is an outbound adapter, so it
    lives in payment-infrastructure — a payment_operations table (V6) reached with a plain
    JdbcTemplate, not a MyBatis persistence module, because this context still owns no persisted
    aggregate and the log needs no mapping.

    It has to be a table, and specifically a table on the shared DataSource. The requirement is not
    durability but co-transactionality: recording a decision and publishing its outcome must commit
    or roll back together, or a claim can outlive the event it was claimed for and the outcome is
    lost permanently. See the PaymentOperations javadoc.
  -->
  <artifactId>payment</artifactId>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>
  <name>payment</name>
  <description>Payment bounded context.</description>

  <modules>
    <module>payment-api</module>
    <module>payment-domain</module>
    <module>payment-application</module>
    <module>payment-infrastructure</module>
    <module>payment-adapter</module>
  </modules>
</project>
